    "devanagari": "स घोषो धार्तराष्ट्राणां हृदयानि व्यदारयत्। नभश्च पृथिवीं चैव तुमुलो व्यनुनादयन्॥1.19॥",
    "iast": "sa ghoṣo dhārtarāṣṭrāṇāṃ hṛdayāni vyadārayat nabhaśca pṛthivīṃ caiva tumulo vyanunādayan",
    "conceptIds": ["gita-visada"],
    "interpretiveNotes": [{"note": "Kashmir Series of Texts and Studies No. LXIV (1943): Bhagavadgītā with Sarvatobhadra of Rājānaka Rāmakantha, ed. Madhusudan Kaul Shastri; Sanskrit-only source, English rendering original to this project."}, {"note": "Rāmakantha versification (PDF p.12, KSTS p.12, scan-verified p22_1819b.png): the edition's 1.19 reads saubhadraś ca mahābāhuḥ śaṅkhān dadhmuḥ pṛthak pṛthak / sa ghoṣo dhārtarāṣṭrāṇāṃ hṛdayāni vyadārayat — i.e. our 1.18b + our 1.19a. Our 1.19b (nabhaś ca pṛthivīṃ…) stands as its 1.20a. We keep vulgate versification for id-stability; the ghoṣa/heart-rending line is identical in both prints."}, {"note": "Sarvatobhadra (PDF pp.15–16, KSTS pp.15–16): verses 1.2–1.19 are nigada-vyākhyāta — self-evident, no word-gloss — their tātparya is karma-ghoratva-pratipādana, and this verse is its verdict: paraspara-vadhādhyavasāya-saṃrabdha-sainya-samāgata-ghora-samara-saṃrambha — the dread muster of hosts resolved on mutual slaughter (p.16)."}],
    "translation": "That proclamation rent the hearts of Dhṛtarāṣṭra's sons — tumultuously echoing through heaven and earth.",
    "commentary": "Plain meaning, fully drawn out: the muster's verdict falls, and the verse divides into inward wound and outward echo. Sa ghoṣo dhārtarāṣṭrāṇāṃ hṛdayāni vyadārayat — 'that ghoṣa tore the hearts of Dhṛtarāṣṭra's sons': saḥ (that — the just-closed Pāṇḍava music of 1.14–1.18 gathered into one demonstrative, distinction's totality), ghoṣaḥ (proclamation, resounding utterance — against 1.13's śabda, mere sound: ghoṣa is sound that declares, the Self-side's answer to noise), dhārtarāṣṭrāṇām (of Dhṛtarāṣṭra's sons — the faction named by blind-fatherhood at last, māmakāḥ of 1.1 grown into patronymic), hṛdayāni (hearts, plural — every breast), vyadārayat (causative imperfect of vi-√dṝ: caused to split open, rent asunder — the verse's only violent verb, and its violence is acoustic: no arrow, only resonance, wounds). Nabhaś ca pṛthivīṃ caiva tumulo vyanunādayan — 'tumultuously filling heaven and earth with echo': nabhaḥ (heaven, sky — aspiration's realm), pṛthivīm (earth — embodiment's ground; the pṛthivī of 1.18's pṛthivīpate now sounding back at its lord), ca…ca…eva (both…and indeed — totality sealed), tumulaḥ (tumultuous — the Kaurava word of 1.13 turned round: their śabda became tumula and stayed effectless; this ghoṣa becomes tumula and rends — same tumult, opposite power), vyanunādayan (present participle of vi-anu-√nad: echoing-after through and through — anu-, after, following: heaven and earth resound *after* the shells, creation answering consciousness). So the antithesis completes: 1.13's tumult deafened its makers (no effect stated), 1.19's tumult wounds its hearers (hearts split) — delusion's noise inflates the breast, truth's proclamation breaks it. Sarvatobhadra meaning, fully drawn out: nigada-vyākhyāta — and Rāmakantha's tātparya-sentence (p.16) names exactly this span: paraspara-vadhādhyavasāya-saṃrabdha-sakala-kuru-pāṇḍava-sainya-samāgata-subhaṭa-ghora-samara-saṃrambha-udyoga-sūcaka — verses 1.2–1.19 signify the furious enterprise of dread battle joined by heroes of both hosts resolved on mutual slaughter. Within that ghora-frame, 1.19 is the reversal-point: karma-ghoratva displayed from the Self-side becomes bhayaṅkara (terror-causing) to the ego before it becomes abhayaṅkara (fear-removing) for the seeker. Hṛdayāni vyadārayat figures bhinna-hṛdaya, the pierced heart — ahaṃkāra's casing (hṛdaya-granthi) split by nāda before it can be healed by jñāna: Arjuna's own heart will break openly at 1.28–1.30 (sīdanti mama gātrāṇi, romaharṣa, gāṇḍīva collapse), and Dhṛtarāṣṭra's sons' hearts break here secretly — the two breakings rhyme, objector and observed sharing one wound, which is why the teaching can address both. Nabhaḥ-pṛthivī echo figures tattva-vyāpti (pervasion of all levels, pṛthivī to khecarī): the shock spans embodiment to aspiration because kriyā-śakti (p.15) spans them — nothing in the manifest is unmoved when recognition sounds. Tumulaḥ-turned-round carries the doctrine's sting: the same word judges both musics — tumult that stays tumult (1.13, effectless, self-deafening) vs. tumult that rends (1.19, effective, other-piercing); ghora belongs to whichever side lacks the Self, and the Kaurava ghora (mass without center) now meets the Pāṇḍava ghora (distinction with center) — and bleeds first. Thus the nineteen-verse tableau closes as Rāmakantha promises (p.16): gatārtham eva, the purpose served — the terrible form of action stands fully displayed, both hosts sounding, one heart breaking — and the śāstra may now turn inward (1.20's kapidhvajaḥ, the Ape-bannered raising his bow) toward the viṣāda that occasions grace.",
    "keyPoints": ["sa ghoṣaḥ: distinction's totality gathered in one word", "ghoṣa vs śabda: proclamation vs mere sound", "dhārtarāṣṭrāṇām: blind-fatherhood named at last", "vyadārayat: war's only violence is acoustic", "nabhaḥ + pṛthivīm: aspiration to embodiment, all levels", "pṛthivī sounds back at its pati (1.18 → 1.19)", "tumulaḥ turned round: same tumult, opposite power", "paraspara-vadhādhyavasāya: Rāmakantha's ghora-verdict (p.16)", "two heart-breakings rhyme: theirs secret (1.19), his open (1.28–30)", "tableau closes (gatārtham): dread displayed, grace may enter"],
    "wordMeaning": "saḥ (demonstrative) — that; ghoṣaḥ (nominative singular masculine, √ghuṣ) — proclamation, resounding utterance; dhārtarāṣṭrāṇām (genitive plural masculine, dhṛtarāṣṭra + aṇ) — of Dhṛtarāṣṭra's sons; hṛdayāni (accusative plural neuter) — hearts; vyadārayat (3rd singular imperfect causative parasmaipada of vi-√dṝ) — rent asunder, tore open; nabhaḥ (accusative singular neuter, here with -s retained as nabhaś before ca) — heaven, sky; ca (conjunction) — and; pṛthivīm (accusative singular feminine) — earth; ca eva (conjunction + emphatic) — and indeed; tumulaḥ (nominative singular masculine) — tumultuous (agreeing with ghoṣaḥ); vyanunādayan (present participle parasmaipada of vi-anu-√nad, nominative singular masculine) — echoing through and after. Rāmakantha 1.19a (in print, = our 1.18b): saubhadraś ca mahābāhuḥ śaṅkhān dadhmuḥ pṛthak pṛthak — see 1.18 wordMeaning. Sandhi: saḥ + ghoṣaḥ → sa ghoṣo (visarga → o before voiced); nabhaḥ + ca → nabhaśca; ca + eva → caiva.",
